# Frontend Core Standards

## Scope and quality

- Prefer the smallest correct change. Do not refactor unrelated code.
- Match existing patterns in the codebase before introducing new abstractions.
- Ship accessible, responsive UI by default — not as a follow-up task.

## Project conventions

- Use the `@/` path alias for imports from `src/`.
- Keep shared UI in `@/components/ui` (shadcn). Add components via `npx shadcn@latest add <name>`.
- Put app-wide providers in `src/providers/`.
- Put reusable hooks in `src/hooks/`, utilities in `src/lib/`.

## Accessibility (a11y)

- Use semantic HTML: `main`, `nav`, `section`, `button`, `label`, etc.
- Every interactive control needs an accessible name (`aria-label`, visible text, or associated `label`).
- Support keyboard navigation and visible focus states.
- Images need meaningful `alt` text; decorative images use `alt=""`.
- Prefer shadcn components — they handle many a11y patterns already.

## Performance

- Avoid unnecessary re-renders: memoize expensive work, don't lift state too early.
- Lazy-load routes and heavy components with `React.lazy` + `Suspense`.
- Keep bundle size in mind: prefer tree-shakeable imports (`lucide-react` named imports).
- Debounce search/filter inputs; paginate or virtualize long lists.

## Error and loading states

- Always handle loading, empty, error, and success states for async UI.
- Show user-friendly error messages; log technical details only in dev tools.
- Never leave users staring at a blank screen during fetches.

## Security

- Never commit secrets. Use `import.meta.env` for Vite env vars (prefix public vars with `VITE_`).
- Sanitize user-generated HTML before rendering (`dangerouslySetInnerHTML` is a last resort).
- Validate and encode data at trust boundaries (forms, URL params, API responses).
